# Template for .env.gluetun — the VPN container's secrets.
#   cp .env.gluetun.example .env.gluetun && chmod 600 .env.gluetun
# The real file is gitignored; this template is tracked. No values here, ever.

# --- ProtonVPN WireGuard key ---------------------------------------------
# The only credential gluetun needs for Proton: the key IS the identity, so
# there is no separate username or password.
#
# Generate at account.protonvpn.com -> Downloads -> WireGuard configuration:
#
#   1. Give the container its OWN key, not the desktop app's. They are
#      independent, so revoking or rotating one leaves the other running.
#   2. Tick NAT-PMP (Port Forwarding) BEFORE generating. This is the one that
#      bites: without it the tunnel comes up perfectly and port forwarding
#      never works. It cannot be added to an existing key — you have to
#      generate a new one. gluetun does say so plainly, so check the logs:
#        ERROR [vpn] starting port forwarding service: ... connection refused
#        - make sure ... your Wireguard key is set to work with PMP
#   3. Pick a server that supports P2P, in the country you set as
#      SERVER_COUNTRIES in deploy/casaos/docker-compose.yml.
#
# Paste only the PrivateKey line from the downloaded .conf (a 44-character
# base64 string ending in "="), not the whole file.
WIREGUARD_PRIVATE_KEY=

# --- PIA account (legacy, unused) ----------------------------------------
# Kept commented for rollback only. gluetun ignores these while VPN_TYPE is
# wireguard; restoring PIA means uncommenting them AND reverting the compose
# file to VPN_SERVICE_PROVIDER/VPN_TYPE/SERVER_REGIONS, since PIA has no
# native WireGuard support in gluetun.
#OPENVPN_USER=
#OPENVPN_PASSWORD=

# --- gluetun control server ----------------------------------------------
# Generate your own, per machine — the laptop and casaos get different keys:
#   docker run --rm qmcgaw/gluetun genkey
# torrent_agent/vpn.py sends this as the control-server API key.
GLUETUN_API_KEY=
